{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'User.create|include\\\\(\\\"posts\\\"\\\\)|db.close|\\\\.create\\\\(' prisma-8.md node_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres -g '*.md' | head -50\"","aggregated_output":"node_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/SKILL.md:70:| Wire the runtime | [`references/runtime.md`](references/runtime.md) | `db.ts`, `contractFromJson<Contract>(...)`, `postgres({ contract })` / `sqlite({ contract })` / `mongo({ contract })` façades, middleware composition (lints, budgets, cache via `@prisma/orm-extension-middleware-cache`, custom `activityEnded` middleware, `@prisma/orm-postgres/family-runtime`), `DATABASE_URL`, `.env`, connection pool / `poolOptions`, dev vs prod config, transactions, read replicas, multi-database, script won't exit / hangs, `db.close` / `pool.end`, `await using` / `[Symbol.asyncDispose]` |\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:134:      `createMongoRuntime()` takes `{ context, adapter, driver }`, as the SQL runtime does: create the adapter with `stack.adapter.create(stack)` and pass it beside the context. The runtime reads the target from `context.contract.target`, so build the context over the validated contract. `MongoExecutionContext` no longer has a `stack` member or a second type parameter, and its contract must be a MongoDB contract, as SQL's `ExecutionContext` requires a SQL contract: write `MongoExecutionContext<Contract>`, and build it with `createMongoExecutionContext({ contract, stack })` over a validated contract. `createMongoExecutionStack()` still takes an optional `driver`; given one, it returns `MongoExecutionStackWithDriver`, whose `driver` is always set, and without one it returns `MongoExecutionStack`, which has no `driver` member, as for SQL. `buildMongoEnums()` is removed: read the contract's enum accessors from `db.enums`, from `mongo({ contract })` or `mongoStatic({ contract })`. `MongoExecutionContext` has no `enums` member. The detection matches files that call `createMongoRuntime(`, read `.stack` from a context, give `MongoExecutionContext` two type arguments or a non-contract type argument, or name `buildMongoEnums` or `MongoEnumAccessors`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:463:const driver = stack.driver.create();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:467:Replace `MongoDriverImpl.fromDb(client.db(dbName))` with `driver.connect({ kind: 'mongoClient', client, dbName })` on a driver created the same way. Code that only sends DDL through a control adapter can use the control driver instead: `await mongoControlDriver.create(url)`, with `mongoControlDriver` the default export of `@prisma/orm-mongo/driver/control`. The control driver uses the database the URL names, so put `dbName` in the URL's path, for example `mongodb://localhost:27017/app`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:512:const runtime = createMongoRuntime({ context, adapter: stack.adapter.create(stack), driver });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:15:- User is running a one-off script (`tsx my-script.ts`, Node CLI, CI task) and the process won't exit after queries finish, or they need script teardown (`db.close()`, `await using`).\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:17:- User mentions: *db.ts, postgres(), mongo(), middleware, lints, budgets, cache, query log, slow query, DATABASE_URL, .env, connection pool, poolOptions, dev vs prod, transactions, read replicas, multi-database, script won't exit, hangs, db.close, db.end, close connection, pool.end, await using, serverless, edge, Cloudflare Workers, Hyperdrive, postgresServerless, connect per request*.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:66:The Mongo façade has the same construction shape — `import mongo from '@internal/mongo/runtime'` — and the same `db.connect(...)` / `db.close()` lifecycle methods. **The Mongo façade does not expose `db.transaction(...)`.** See *What Prisma 8 doesn't do yet* for the workaround. **The ORM surface differs in one place: keys.** On Mongo, `db.orm` is keyed by the collection's storage name (from `@@map(...)`, or the lowercased model name if no `@@map` is set), not by the PSL model name — so `model User { … @@map(\"users\") }` is reached at `db.orm.users`, not `db.orm.public.User`. The SQL builder lane (`db.sql.<ns>.<table>`) doesn't exist on Mongo at all (`db.sql` is `undefined`). See `references/queries.md` § *MongoDB ORM addressing* for the full rule and a rewrite recipe for SQL-target examples.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:70:The concept: short scripts that connect, query, then expect the process to exit will **hang on Postgres** because the `pg.Pool` owned by `postgres()` keeps Node's event loop alive. The data round-trip succeeds; the script never exits. Call `await db.close()` before the script returns (or use `await using` **at the top of a script module** so teardown runs when the module exits — see the block-scope warning below for why this matters).\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:78:const created = await db.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com', name: 'Alice' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:82:await db.close();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:96:// db.close() runs automatically when the script module exits.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:129:Servers (HTTP handlers, workers in a request loop) **do not call `db.close()`** at all in steady state. The pool stays open for the process lifetime. `db.close()` and `await using` are for short-lived scripts — `tsx my-script.ts`, Node CLI commands, CI tasks, one-off seed runs — not for code that runs inside a request loop.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:136:- **Ownership.** `close()` releases only what the façade constructed (`pg.Pool` from `{ url }`, `MongoClient` from `{ url }` / `{ uri, dbName }`, SQLite handle from `{ path }`). If you supplied your own `pg.Pool` / `pg.Client` (Postgres `pg:` option), `mongodb.MongoClient` (Mongo `mongoClient:` option), or a pre-built `binding`, `db.close()` does **not** touch those — you own their lifecycle.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:138:**`db.end()` does not exist.** The universal `node-postgres` name is `pool.end()` on a `pg.Pool`; the Prisma 8 runtime client is not a `pg.Pool`. The right call is `await db.close()`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:166:The rule to remember: inside a request, `db` does everything the `db` from `postgres()` does, so any documented `db.orm...`, `db.sql...`, `db.raw...`, `db.transaction(...)`, `db.prepare(...)` or `db.query(...)` snippet works unchanged. Await every query before the `await using` scope ends. The connection's `close()` waits until the runtime has been idle for one turn of the event loop (a `setTimeout(0)`), then refuses new work. A lazy ORM read such as `db.orm.public.User.all()`, or `db.query(plan)`, returned from the scope without `await` starts only when the caller awaits it, after the scope has closed, so it rejects with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Runtime is closed\"), whose `fix` names the missing `await`. Work that keeps the runtime busy from the close onward completes: a `first()`, `execute()` or `transaction()`, an ORM write and its reload, including `include(...).create()` and a nested `create()`, and an async helper whose steps go through `db.orm`, `db.transaction()` or `db.prepare()`; a transaction commits. Such work is never refused because of the close; if it fails for its own reason, it rejects like any unawaited promise. A helper that calls `db.runtime()` after the scope has ended throws \"Postgres connection is closed\" at that call. Work that starts after the runtime was idle for a turn is refused. Whether a query sent after waiting on something other than the database counts as idle depends on when that callback runs relative to the timer, so do not rely on it. An unawaited promise that fails then, or fails for its own reason, rejects like any unawaited promise, and Node reports it as unhandled. Write `return await db.orm...`, not `return db.orm...`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:173:- **After `db.close()`** (or the end of the `await using` scope), `db.runtime()` throws `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Postgres connection is closed\"), and ORM queries, `db.transaction(...)` and prepared statements that start after the runtime was idle for a turn reject with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Runtime is closed\"). Call `postgres.connect({ url })` again for a new connection.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:364:  const user = await tx.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:365:  await tx.orm.public.Post.create({ userId: user.id, title: 'hello' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:415:`path` is optional at construct time (you can call `db.connect({ path })` later); omit it and the façade still returns a client. The SQLite façade exposes the same `db.sql`, `db.orm`, `db.raw`, `db.prepare(...)`, `db.transaction(...)`, `db.close()`, and `[Symbol.asyncDispose]` surfaces as Postgres, with one addressing difference: SQLite has no schemas, so `db.sql` and `db.orm` are the unbound namespace itself (`db.orm.User`, `db.sql.user`) instead of Postgres's `db.orm.public.User` / `db.sql.public.user`. `db.orm` and `tx.orm` also offer `fragment` beside the models, unless a model is named `fragment`: `const titled = db.orm.fragment({ title: { codecId: 'sqlite/text@1', nullable: false } }, (rows) => rows.where((r) => r.title.eq('Hello')));` then `await db.orm.Post.with(titled).all();`. The Mongo façade shares `db.orm`, `db.close()`, and `[Symbol.asyncDispose]` but has no `db.sql` and no `db.transaction(...)`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:437:8. **Script hangs after queries finish on Postgres.** The `pg.Pool` keeps Node's event loop alive. Solution: `await db.close()` before the script returns, or `await using db = postgres({ contract, ... })` at the top of a script module. Do not put `await using db = postgres(...)` inside a request handler — it's block-scoped and would close the pool after every request. The right server pattern is a module-level singleton in `db.ts` that lives for the process lifetime. On a per-request runtime, use `postgresServerless()` and `await using db = await postgres.connect({ url })` in the handler instead (see *Workflow — Serverless and per-request runtimes*).\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.22-to-8.0.0-rc.23/instructions.md:93:      A MongoDB migration creates and drops each index by its name. `prisma migration plan` writes the name into `migration.ts`, as in `createIndex(\"users\", [{ field: \"email\", direction: 1 }], { name: \"User_email_key\", unique: true })` and `dropIndex(\"users\", [{ field: \"email\", direction: 1 }], { name: \"User_email_key\", unique: true })`. The operation id is `index.<collection>.<create|drop>(<name>)`, such as `index.users.create(email_1)`, where it was `index.users.create(email:1)`. A `migration.ts` written by an earlier release keeps working: an index without a name is named after its keys, as before.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.22-to-8.0.0-rc.23/instructions.md:414:A script or test that matches an operation id, such as `index.users.create(email:1)`, matches the name instead: `index.users.create(email_1)`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:89:const user = await db.orm.users.create({\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.19-to-8.0.0-rc.20/instructions.md:156:      `db.close()` on a Supabase client now refuses new work at once on every role-bound client and on `.supabase`, with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` \"Runtime is closed\", and waits for work already running, open role transactions included, before it ends the pool. With a pool you passed as `pg`, a query after `close()` used to throw at the call; its promise now rejects instead. Await every role transaction before `close()`, or `close()` waits for it.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.19-to-8.0.0-rc.20/instructions.md:504:await db.close();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:308:const user = await db.orm.public.User.create({ id, email, displayName, kind, createdAt });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:313:  .create({ id, email, displayName, kind, createdAt });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:618:  const user = await tx.orm.public.User.create({ id, email });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:619:  await tx.orm.public.Post.create({ userId: user.id, title: 'hello' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:666:const user = await db.orm.public.User.create({ id: 1, email: 'a@x.io' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:667:const authUser = await db.orm.auth.User.create({ id: 2, token: 'tok' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:263:      On a client that closes at once (`postgres({ contract, url })`, `sqlite()`, `supabase()`), an ORM call that resolves to one value, such as `first()`, `create()`, `include(...).create()` or a nested create, starts when it is called, and `close()` waits for it, so one left pending at `close()` now completes instead of rejecting: a `create()` used to reject with \"Runtime is closed\", and `include(...).create()` and a nested create with \"Postgres client is closed\". A streaming read such as `all()` starts when it is read; read after `close()`, it rejects with \"Postgres client is closed\" instead of \"Runtime is closed\". Update code or tests that expect the old outcome.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1026:await db.orm.public.Post.create({ title: 'Hello', publishedAt: new Date() as unknown as string });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1029:await db.orm.public.Post.create({ title: 'Hello', publishedAt: new Date().toISOString() });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1426:- await db.orm.User.create({ id: '123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000' as Char<36>, email });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1428:+ await db.orm.User.create({ id: '123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000', email });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1550:const user = await db.orm.users.create({ name: 'Alice' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1555:const user = await db.orm.users.create({ name: 'Alice' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/supabase.md:125:Options beyond the basics: `middleware` (same composition as `postgres()` — see `references/runtime.md`; middleware never sees the role-binding `set_config` calls), `poolOptions`, `pg` (BYO `pg.Pool` instead of `url`; a `pg.Client` is rejected with `SUPABASE.CONFIG_INVALID`). Teardown is `await db.close()` / `await using` exactly as in `references/runtime.md` — the same script-hang rules apply.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.11-to-8.0.0-rc.12/instructions.md:728:const familyInstance = family.create(stack);\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.20-to-8.0.0-rc.21/instructions.md:326:await db.close();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.20-to-8.0.0-rc.21/instructions.md:356:await db.close();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:108:When the user is running a one-off `tsx my-script.ts` (not a long-lived server), call `await db.close()` at the end so the process exits cleanly — on Postgres the façade-owned pool keeps Node's event loop alive; on Mongo the façade-owned `MongoClient` does the same. See `references/runtime.md` § *Running as a script (teardown)* for the full pattern including `await using`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:116:  await db.orm.public.User.create(u);\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:121://   await db.orm.users.create(u);\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:125:await db.close();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/debug.md:70:| `TypeError: db.end is not a function` | The runtime client does not expose `db.end()` — that's the `node-postgres` pool API (`pool.end()`). The right call is `await db.close()`. See `references/runtime.md` § *Running as a script (teardown)*. |\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/debug.md:71:| Script hangs after queries print / process won't exit | On Postgres the façade-owned `pg.Pool` keeps the event loop alive. Call `await db.close()` before the script returns, or `await using db = postgres({ contract, ... })` at the top of a script module (do NOT put `await using` inside a request handler — block-scoped, would close per-request). See `references/runtime.md` § *Running as a script (teardown)*. |\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/debug.md:72:| `Error('Postgres client is closed')` / `Error('SQLite client is closed')` / `Error('Mongo client is closed')` | The client was closed via `db.close()` (terminal state). Remove the early `close()`, reorder so `close()` runs last after all queries, or construct a new `db` if reconnection is intended. See `references/runtime.md` § *Running as a script (teardown)*. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'include\\\\(|relation|create\\\\(' node_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md | head -35\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'include\\\\(|relation|create\\\\(' node_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md | head -35\"","aggregated_output":"6:Once the contract is emitted and the DB is up to date, this skill covers everything you do *with* the data: reading, writing, eager-loading relations, aggregating, and the choice between the ORM and the lower-level query lane.\n11:- User wants to include / eager-load relations.\n116:  await db.orm.public.User.create(u);\n121://   await db.orm.users.create(u);\n172:The model is the whole row plus its relations, and each related model carries its own relations in turn, so no query returns a value of the model type. A query returns the fields it fetched. The default fetch returns `Scalars<Model>`, the model without relations: `db.orm.public.User.first()` returns `Scalars<Model> | null`, and `db.orm.public.User.all()` returns `Scalars<Model>[]` (or its async iterable). Four types cover every case, and none needs a client in scope:\n174:- `Models.<ns>_<Model>` (from `contract.d.ts`) — every scalar field and every relation. On SQLite, which has no schemas, the name is bare: `Models.User` and `typeof models.User`. On Postgres the schema is part of the name: `Models.public_User`, or `typeof models.public.User` by dotted access — a model declared outside any `namespace { }` block is in `public`, so that is also its name. Mongo names its models the same way. A polymorphic base also emits one member per variant and an `Any<Base>` union (`Models.public_AnyTask`).\n175:- `Scalars<M>` — the model without relations; what a default fetch returns. Distributes over unions, so `Scalars<Models.public_AnyTask>` is the union of variant rows.\n176:- `Shape<M, Spec>` — a data structure derived from the model, for declaring an endpoint's response type once and having the compiler check the body at the `return`. At every level of `Spec`: `'+'` is a union of scalar and relation names to keep (a relation named there comes with all of its scalars and none of its relations; the scalars are narrowed only when `'+'` names a scalar, so `'+': 'posts'` alone is every scalar plus posts); `'-'` is a union of scalar names to drop; `'+'` naming a scalar beside `'-'` is a compile error, while `{ '-': 'passwordHash'; '+': 'posts' }` is every scalar but the hash plus posts; any other key is a relation whose value is a nested spec that narrows the related model. Relations are absent unless asked for; `X[]`, `X | null`, or `X` comes from the model. Wrong names, a relation in `'-'`, a non-object relation value, and a relation both in `'+'` and as a key are compile errors. No `where`/`orderBy`/`limit`; compose extras with TypeScript (`Shape<M> & { postCount: number }`).\n177:- `ResultType<typeof query>` — the row of any ORM collection value (plain, `.include()`, `.select()`, `.variant()`), and of SQL lane plans. Bind the query to a name first; `typeof` needs a value. `.variant()` takes the variant's discriminator value, not its model name: `db.orm.public.Task.variant('bug')` for `@@base(Task, \"bug\")`. Call it once, on the base collection; a second `.variant()` on a variant collection is refused.\n187:const usersWithTasks = () => db.orm.public.User.include('tasks');\n198:    .include('posts', (posts) => posts.include('tags'))\n205:// @ts-expect-error 'nope' is not a relation of User\n209:On Mongo the imports are `@prisma/orm-mongo/family-contract/types` and `@prisma/orm-mongo/components/runtime`; embedded documents are fields, so they stay in `Scalars`. To name a model plus some of its relations, write `Shape<Models.public_User, { '+': 'id' | 'posts' }>`, not `Pick<Models.public_User, 'id' | 'posts'>`: `Pick` demands fully loaded nested posts that no query returns. Input types (`CreateInput<Contract, 'User'>`, `MutationUpdateInput<Contract, 'User'>`, `ShorthandWhereFilter<Contract, 'public', 'User'>`) come from `@prisma/orm-postgres/orm-client`.\n211:Coming from Prisma 7: `Prisma.User` → `Models.public_User` (note: now carries relations; the scalars-only row is `Scalars<Models.public_User>`); `Prisma.UserGetPayload<{ include: { posts: true } }>` → `Shape<Models.public_User, { '+': 'posts' }>`; `Prisma.UserGetPayload<{ select: { id: true; posts: { select: { title: true } } } }>` → `Shape<Models.public_User, { '+': 'id'; posts: { '+': 'title' } }>`; `Prisma.UserCreateInput` → `CreateInput<Contract, 'User'>`; `Awaited<ReturnType<typeof fn>>` → `ResultType<typeof query>`.\n213:## Ordering by a relation (SQL targets)\n215:Inside an ORM `.orderBy(...)`: a to-one relation's field (`(p) => p.author.name.asc()`), a to-many relation's count (`(u) => u.posts.count().desc()`), a filtered count (`(u) => u.posts.count((p) => p.views.gt(10)).desc()`), and null placement on any `.asc()` / `.desc()` (`(u) => u.invitedBy.name.desc({ nulls: 'last' })`). One hop. `cursor()` rejects these orders with `ORM.ARGUMENT_INVALID`. Details in [`queries-postgres.md`](./queries-postgres.md). Mongo's `.orderBy({ field: 1 | -1 })` has neither.\n227:- **Many-to-many relations work through the junction.** `.include('tags', (tag) => tag.select(...))` traverses an N:M relation's `through` table, and nested `create` / `connect` / `disconnect` on an N:M relation write the junction rows for you (`examples/prisma-8-demo/src/orm-client/get-post-tags.ts`, `create-post-with-tags.ts`). The one refusal: a junction with required payload columns the relation API cannot populate throws `ORM.RELATION_MUTATION_UNSUPPORTED` — write that junction directly or use the SQL builder.\n236:- **Automatic N+1 detection.** Prisma 8 does not warn when an `.include(...)` is missing. Workaround: be deliberate about `.include(...)` in code review; the `lints` middleware (see `references/runtime.md`) catches the more common authoring slips (missing `WHERE` on a `DELETE` / `UPDATE`, missing `LIMIT` on a `SELECT`).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
